<h3>The Sun News-Pictorial</h3>
<p>Keith joined <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Herald_and_Weekly_Times">The Herald and Weekly Times</a> in 1946 and spent more than three decades at its flagship tabloid, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Sun_News-Pictorial">The Sun News-Pictorial</a>, where from 1958 he wrote the daily 'A Place in the Sun' column — a Melbourne institution that had run since the paper's first edition in 1922. The column was a direct line into the texture of Australian suburban life: wine culture finding its feet, the demolition of old Melbourne, social fashions from Toorak to the outer suburbs, and the thousand small peculiarities that made the city what it was. At a time when most Australians got their picture of the world from a single morning paper, 'A Place in the Sun' was where a generation of Melburnians read about themselves.</p>
