<h3>The Bulletin</h3>
<p><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Bulletin_(Australian_periodical)">The Bulletin</a> was Australia's oldest and most influential opinion weekly, founded in 1880 and a cradle of the national literary identity through Henry Lawson, Banjo Paterson and generations of satirists and commentators who followed them. Keith wrote for it from 1962 to 1984, initially under the pseudonym 'Batman' — a nod to the magazine's tradition of anonymous contributors — filing a column called 'Around Melbourne' that became one of its most-read regular features. Where The Sun gave him daily breadth, The Bulletin gave him the space to be caustic, digressive and funny at greater length, and the column's subjects — football tribalism, pub culture, the sociology of Melbourne's suburbs, the slow vanishing of the old city — add up to an unusually candid social record of the era.</p>
