<h3>The Age</h3>
<p>Keith joined <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Age">The Age</a> in 1984 after nearly four decades at The Herald and Weekly Times, a move that felt to him — and to observers — like a defection of the order of Ron Barassi leaving Melbourne for Carlton. His columns ran until 2013, were regularly reprinted in the Sydney Morning Herald and Canberra Times, and two collections drawn from them became books: 'No Brains on Tuesday' and 'A Day in the Life of Australia', the latter commissioned for the bicentennial. The Age gave his writing a slightly more reflective register than The Sun had demanded — the columns from this period are more essayistic, more willing to sit with a subject and turn it over — and together they form the most complete record we have of his final three decades as a working journalist.</p>
