<h3>The Courier-Mail</h3>
<p><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Courier-Mail">The Courier-Mail</a> has been Queensland's newspaper of record since 1933, and for most of that time it was the dominant daily voice of a state that considered itself culturally distinct from the southern capitals — more outdoor, more provincial, less encumbered by the old-money conventions that Keith spent much of his career gently lampooning. His contributions were occasional rather than regular, pieces that came when a story or a visit took him north, and they tend to read as a Melburnian's affectionate but clear-eyed dispatches from another country: the Gold Coast's breakneck reinvention of itself, Queensland's particular relationship with heat, leisure and the public beach, and the gap between the two cities' understanding of what constituted the good life.</p>
